From: Stanislaw Pal <kuncy7@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] usb: dwc3: qcom: select the TCSR phy mux before the controller reset

On IPQ5018 and IPQ5332 the USB SuperSpeed pads are shared with PCIe
through a mux in TCSR (0x01947540 on both SoCs). The bootloader does
not necessarily select the USB position: GL.iNet's U-Boot happens to
leave it right on the Flint 3, TP-Link's on the Archer AX55 v1 never
touches it, so without this the SS link never leaves Rx.Detect there
while USB2 keeps working (high speed does not go through the mux).

Ordering is the critical part. The mux has to be written BEFORE the
controller goes through its reset in probe: selecting it later - even
before dwc3_qcom_of_register_core() - leaves the SS pads muxed away for
the whole reset and the link is dead for good. This is also what the
vendor (QSDK) dwc3-qcom driver does: dwc3_qcom_phy_sel() is the first
thing in probe. The error path is a plain return, the clocks are not up
yet at this point.

The register is described by an optional DT property, so boards whose
bootloader already selects USB (or SoCs without the mux) need nothing:

	qcom,phy-mux-regs = <&tcsr 0x10540>;

against the TCSR syscon node. Goes on top of the qcom-uniphy-usb PHY
series; the PHY driver itself needs no changes for this.

Tested on a TP-Link Archer AX55 v1 (IPQ5018): a USB 3.2 gen 1 stick
enumerates at 5 Gbps and sustains 112-116 MB/s, both cold boot and
reboot. Same TCSR offset confirmed on IPQ5332 by Dusknoir.

Signed-off-by: Stanislaw Pal <kuncy7@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-qcom.c
@@ -22,6 +22,8 @@
 #include <linux/iopoll.h>
 #include <linux/usb/hcd.h>
 #include <linux/usb.h>
+#include <linux/mfd/syscon.h>
+#include <linux/regmap.h>
 #include "core.h"
 
 /* USB QSCRATCH Hardware registers */
@@ -730,6 +732,37 @@ static int dwc3_qcom_of_register_core(st
 	return ret;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Some IPQ SoCs (e.g. IPQ5018) route the USB pads through a PHY mux
+ * controlled from a TCSR register; the bootloader does not necessarily
+ * select the USB position. Optional "qcom,phy-mux-regs" = <&tcsr offset>
+ * points at it; the vendor driver writes 1 ("usb phy mux sel") there.
+ */
+static int dwc3_qcom_setup_phy_mux(struct dwc3_qcom *qcom)
+{
+	struct of_phandle_args args;
+	struct regmap *map;
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = of_parse_phandle_with_fixed_args(qcom->dev->of_node,
+					       "qcom,phy-mux-regs", 1, 0,
+					       &args);
+	if (ret)
+		return 0;	/* optional */
+
+	map = syscon_node_to_regmap(args.np);
+	of_node_put(args.np);
+	if (IS_ERR(map))
+		return dev_err_probe(qcom->dev, PTR_ERR(map),
+				     "failed to map phy mux regs\n");
+
+	ret = regmap_write(map, args.args[0], 0x1);
+	if (ret)
+		dev_err(qcom->dev, "failed to select USB phy mux: %d\n", ret);
+
+	return ret;
+}
+
 static int dwc3_qcom_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 {
 	struct device_node	*np = pdev->dev.of_node;
@@ -746,6 +779,18 @@ static int dwc3_qcom_probe(struct platfo
 	platform_set_drvdata(pdev, qcom);
 	qcom->dev = &pdev->dev;
 
+	/*
+	 * Select the USB position of the phy mux before anything comes out
+	 * of reset. The vendor driver does this as the first thing in probe,
+	 * ahead of the controller reset and the clocks; selecting it later
+	 * leaves the SuperSpeed pads muxed away while the controller resets,
+	 * and the SS link then never leaves Rx.Detect (USB2 is unaffected,
+	 * it does not go through this mux).
+	 */
+	ret = dwc3_qcom_setup_phy_mux(qcom);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
 	qcom->resets = devm_reset_control_array_get_optional_exclusive(dev);
 	if (IS_ERR(qcom->resets)) {
 		return dev_err_probe(&pdev->dev, PTR_ERR(qcom->resets),
